################# DESCRIPTION OF FLAGS ##########################
#
# This file uses Hunspell flags: flags are case-sensitive.
# In the .dic file, a word/lemma uses flags like:
#   lemma/PSOb   (multiple flags are concatenated; no spaces)
# Example:
#   lya/PS      -> present/very-near-past subject markers (affirmative)
#
# ------------------------- VERBS (building blocks) -------------------------
# PS = Subject markers (affirmative): present simple + very near past
# NP = Subject markers (affirmative): near past
# DP = Subject markers (affirmative): distant past
# NF = Subject markers (affirmative): near future
# FF = Subject markers (affirmative): far future
#
# Ob = Object markers (basic)
# OR = Special reflexive object markers used in constructions like "-mwe-", "-gwe-", etc.
#
# RP = Reflexive verb form subject markers: present simple tense (also usable for some past forms)
# Rn = Reflexive verb form subject markers: near future
# RF = Reflexive verb form subject markers: far future
#
# ps = Negative subject markers: present simple + very near past
# np = Negative subject markers: near + distant past
# nf = Negative subject markers: near future
# ff = Negative subject markers: far future
#
# rp = Negation for reflexive verb forms in present/very-near past (e.g. seetaaga/te-...)
# rf = Reflexive negative subject markers: far future
#
# gg = Infinitive prefix for non-reflexive verbs (oku-/ku- plus agreement variants)
# hh = Infinitive prefix for reflexive verbs (okwee-/kwee- plus agreement variants)
#
# ---------------------- VERBS (generated cross-products) -------------------
# These are pre-generated “subject+object chaining” prefixes to avoid multi-step chaining.
# (They are produced by scripts; see the nearby “Cross product ...” blocks.)
#
# NN = Cross product PS x Ob (present/very-near-past subject + basic object)
# GG = Cross product NP x Ob (near-past subject + basic object)
# QQ = Cross product NF x Ob (near-future subject + basic object)
# RR = Cross product FF x Ob (far-future subject + basic object)
#
# SS = Cross product ps x Ob (present/very-near-past negative subject + basic object)
# II = Cross product np x Ob (past negative subject + basic object)
# WW = Cross product nf x Ob (near-future negative subject + basic object)
# TT = Cross product ff x Ob (far-future negative subject + basic object)
#
# MM = Cross product gg x Ob (infinitive + basic object)
#
# UU = Cross product PS x OR (present/very-near-past subject + special reflexive object)
# VV = Cross product NP x OR (near-past subject + special reflexive object)
# XX = Cross product DP x OR (distant-past subject + special reflexive object)
# YY = Cross product NF x OR (near-future subject + special reflexive object)
# ZZ = Cross product FF x OR (far-future subject + special reflexive object)
#
# --------------------------- NOUNS (noun classes) --------------------------
# Na = Class I noun prefixes
# Nb = Class II noun prefixes
# Nc = Class III noun prefixes
# Nd = Class IV noun prefixes
# Ne = Class V noun prefixes
# Nf = Class VI noun prefixes
# Ng = Class VII noun prefixes
# Nh = Class VIII noun prefixes (large stuff)
# Ni = Class IX noun prefixes
# Nj = Class X noun prefixes
#
# yy = Impersonal possessive prefix
# zz = Size-based noun prefixes (aka-/obu-/ebya-/etc.)
# ------------------------------ QUICK EXAMPLES -----------------------------
# Example .dic entries (illustrative):
#   NOTE: these examples assume the stem exists in your .dic and matches the rule conditions.
#   NOTE: avoid mixing tense flags on one entry; use separate entries per tense.
#
# VERBS (affirmative, subject-only):
#   lya/PS      -> present tense subject prefixes + lya (e.g. ndya, olya, alya, tulya, mulya, balya, ...)
#   lidde/PS    -> very-near-past tense subject prefixes + lidde (e.g. ndidde, olidde, alidde, tulidde, mulidde, balidde, ...)
#   lidde/NP    -> near-past subject prefixes + lidde (e.g. nna+lidde, wa+lidde, ya+lidde, baa+lidde, ...)
#   lya/DP      -> distant-past subject prefixes + lya (e.g. nna+lya, wa+lya, ya+lya, baa+lya, ...)
#   lya/NF      -> near-future subject prefixes + lya (e.g. naa+lya, munaa+lya, onaa+lya, banaa+lya, ...)
#   lya/FF      -> far-future subject prefixes + lya (e.g. ndi+lya, oli+lya, ali+lya, tuli+lya, muli+lya, bali+lya, ...)
#
# VERBS (negative, subject-only):
#   lya/ps      -> present/very-near-past negatives (e.g. si+lya, to+lya, ta+lya, tetu+lya, temu+lya, teba+lya, ...)
#   lidde/ps    -> present/very-near-past negatives (e.g. si+lidde, to+lidde, ta+lidde, tetu+lidde, temu+lidde, teba+lidde, ...)
#   lya/np      -> near+distant past negatives (e.g. saalya, tewalya, teyalya, tetwalya, temwalya, tebaalya, ...)
#   lidde/np    -> near past negatives (e.g. saalidde, tewalidde, teyalidde, tetwalidde, temwalidde, tebaalidde, ...)
#   lye/nf      -> near-future negatives (e.g. siilye, temuulye, toolye, taalye, tetuulye, temuulye, tebaalye, ...)
#   lya/ff      -> far-future negatives (e.g. sililya, tolilya, talilya, tetulilya, temulilya, tebalilya, ...)
#
# VERBS (infinitives):
#   lya/gg      -> infinitive forms (oku+lya, ku+lya, and possessives like ow'oku+lya, ...)
#   naaza/hh    -> reflexive infinitive forms (okwee+naaza, kwee+naaza, and possessives like ow'okwee+naaza, ...)
#
# VERBS (subject+object chaining):
#   Use the generated cross-product flags when you want subject+object without relying on multi-step prefix chaining.
#   lya/NN               -> present/very-near-past subject+basic-object (PS x Ob) e.g. mbilya, tuzilya, zitulya, abilidde, zibilidde
#   lidde/GG             -> near-past subject+basic-object (NP x Ob) e.g.  wabilidde, nnakilidde, gwaakilidde
#   lya/QQ               -> near-future subject+basic-object (NF x Ob) e.g. munaakilya, onaakilya, abanakilya
#   lya/RR               -> far-future subject+basic-object (FF x Ob) e.g. olikalya, mulibilya, balikalya
#   lya/SS or lidde/SS   -> present/very-near-past negative subject+basic-object (ps x Ob) e.g. obutagulya, sibilidde
#   lya/MM               -> infinitive+basic-object (gg x Ob) e.g. okuzilya, okubulya
#
# VERBS (special reflexive object markers like -mwe-/-gwe-):
#   kutte/UU or liisa     -> present/very-near-past subject + special reflexive object (PS x OR) e.g. ngwekutte, nzeeliisa, olwekutte
#   kwata/VV or kutte     -> near-past and far past subject + special reflexive object (NP x OR) e.g. nnabeekwata, nnabeekutte
#   liisa/ZZ                -> far-future subject + special reflexive object (FF x OR) e.g ndikeeliisa, olikeeliisa
#
# NOUNS:
#   taata/Na    -> noun-class prefixes (e.g. pluralization like ba+taata depending on the noun class rules)
# Na = Class I noun prefixes (omu-/omw- → aba-/ba-, plus some bare stems → ba-)
#      e.g. omuntu → abantu; omwana → abaana; taata → bataata
# Nb = Class II noun prefixes (omu-/omw- → emi-/mi-)
#      e.g. omuti → emiti; omwezi → emyezi
# Nc = Class III noun prefixes (e-/em-)
#      e.g. mayiro → emmayiro
# Nd = Class IV noun prefixes (eki-/eky- → ebi-/eby-)
#      e.g. ekitabo → ebitabo; ekyuma → ebyuma
# Ne = Class V noun prefixes (e-, eri-, ejj-, ery- → ama-/ma-)
#      e.g. eggi → amagi; erinnyo → amannyo
# Nf = Class VI noun prefixes (aka- → obu-/bu-)
#      e.g. akatabo → obutabo
# Ng = Class VII noun prefixes (olu-/olul-/olw- → en-/em-/enn-/enny- etc.)
#      e.g. olulimi → ennimi; olubuto → embuto; olwendo → ennyendo; oluggi → enziggi
# Nh = Class VIII noun prefixes (large stuff: omu-/omw- → ogu-/ogw-; some variants → aga-)
#      e.g. omuntu → oguntu
# Ni = Class IX noun prefixes (oku- nouns: oku- may drop, or pluralize with ama-/ma-)
#      e.g. okutu → kutu; okutu → amatu; okugulu → magulu
# Nj = Class X noun prefixes (otw-/otu- nouns; this rule encodes the allowed alternation)
#      e.g. otulo → tulo
# yy = Impersonal possessive prefix (e.g. ekitabo ky'abaana, eby'abaana)
# zz = Size-based noun prefixes (e.g. akamotoka, obumotoka, olwana)
#
# NOTE: For each word, you have to include the original stem 
# and its respective modified stems in the .dic file and apply only the applicable flags
# For example for the verb "lya" the entries might be:
# lya/PS      (apply PS subject markers for present tense)
# lya/NP      (apply NP subject markers for near past)
# lya/NN      (apply pre-generated PS x Ob cross product for subject+object)
# lidde/PS    (alternative stem form with PS markers)
# liisa/UU    (alternative stem form with special reflexive objects)
################# END OF DESCRIPTION OF FLAGS ##########################

SET UTF8
FLAG long
LANG lg
TRY abcdefghijklmnoprstuvwyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPRSTUVWYZ
BREAK ' 
# WORDCHARS '’
INCONV 1
INCONV  ’ '
NEEDAFFIX NA

# suffix "ko" in words like okulyako, okulabako, fumbako
# Was using flag A but now uses ko
SFX ko Y 1
SFX ko 0 ko [aeiou]

# suffix "mu" in words like tunulamu
# Was using flag O but now uses mu
SFX mu Y 1
SFX mu 0 mu [aeiou]

# suffix "yooko" e.g. tunulayooko
SFX yk Y 2
SFX yk 0 yooko ^yo
SFX yk yo yooko yo # faayo-->faayooko

# suffix "yo" e.g. gendayo
SFX yo Y 1
SFX yo 0 yo [aeiou]

# suffix "wo" e.g. tunulawo
SFX wo Y 1
SFX wo 0 wo [aeiou]

# vowels at the end of possesives e.g. "we" "abe" ke in sentence like abaana bo
SFX ae Y 3
SFX ae 0 a [^aeiou]
SFX ae 0 e [^aeiou]
SFX ae 0 o [^aeiou]

# A person who does an act e.g. omuli, omukubi, omwambazi
# It can also be used like the Luganda equivalent of English's "just"
# e.g. okulaba obulabi, kulya buli
# Was using flag o but now uses oo
PFX oo Y 38
PFX oo o 0 om[uw]
PFX oo omu ba omu
PFX oo omu aba omu
PFX oo omu mi omu
PFX oo omu emi omu
PFX oo omw emy omw
PFX oo omul nd omul.[^mn]
PFX oo omul end omul.[^mn] #e.g. omuli-->endi
PFX oo omul nn omul.[mn]
PFX oo omul enn omul.[mn]
PFX oo omu n omu
PFX oo omu en omu
PFX oo omw ny omw
PFX oo omw eny omw
PFX oo omu ki omu
PFX oo omu eki omu
PFX oo omw ky omw
PFX oo omw eky omw
PFX oo omu bi omu
PFX oo omu ebi omu
PFX oo omw by omw
PFX oo omw eby omw
PFX oo omul dd omul
PFX oo omul edd omul #omulamuoi-->eddamuzi
PFX oo omu ek omuk # omukubi-->ekkubi
PFX oo omw emy omw #e.g. omwambazi-->emyambazi
PFX oo omu ma om[uw]
PFX oo omu ama om[uw] #omulamuzi-->eddamuzi
PFX oo omu ka omu
PFX oo omu aka omu
PFX oo omw ka omw
PFX oo omw aka omw
PFX oo om ob om[uw] # omuli-->obuli, omwambazi-->obwambazi
PFX oo om b  om[uw]
PFX oo om lu om[uw]
PFX oo om olu om[uw]
PFX oo om ok om[uw]
PFX oo om ot om[uw]


# Reflexive verb form, subject markers in present simple tense 
# I think these can also work for the very near past, near and distant past tenses e.g beezimbira, beezimbidde
# Was using flag RD but now uses RP
PFX RP Y 35
PFX RP 0 nee .
PFX RP 0 wee .
PFX RP 0 yee .
PFX RP 0 twe .
PFX RP 0 mwe .
